{
  "directions": [
    "Add to the Offer letters rules: when the hiring approval shows no sign-on bonus or no equity, leave that line out of the letter entirely and never write a £0 bonus or 0 shares, in the same way the relocation block and its heading are removed when the package does not apply. Three letters in this batch kept such a line and each was rejected for it."
  ],
  "findings": [
    {
      "confidence": "high",
      "statement": "The saved letter contains a compensation row filled with a zero value in three of the four sessions, and each of the three has negative feedback naming that row. ses_01a1c4c6 saved Sign-on Bonus: £0 beside a 400 share equity grant, ses_01a1c542 saved Equity Grant: 0 shares beside a £3,000 bonus, and ses_01a1ce2e saved both zero rows. The fourth letter, ses_01a1c5f4, has £4,000 and 800 shares, both rows filled with real values, and its feedback is positive, so the filling, naming, dates and formatting are otherwise correct.",
      "traceIds": [
        "ses_01a1c4c6",
        "ses_01a1c542",
        "ses_01a1ce2e",
        "ses_01a1c5f4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "confidence": "high",
      "statement": "The agent saw the zero rows in its own verification output and kept them. In ses_01a1c4c6 and ses_01a1c542 the extracted letter text printed the zero row while the reply said all placeholders were filled, and in ses_01a1ce2e the reply itself listed the £0 sign-on and 0 shares. The cause is the instruction the agent followed: the current rules handle the relocation block conditionally but say nothing about a compensation row whose approval value is zero, so the unconditional template rows were treated as mandatory.",
      "traceIds": [
        "ses_01a1c4c6",
        "ses_01a1c542",
        "ses_01a1ce2e"
      ]
    },
    {
      "confidence": "high",
      "statement": "The defect recurred after feedback had already named it in an earlier session in the same batch. The feedback on ses_01a1c542 says the same thing happened with equity, pointing back to the £0 bonus in ses_01a1c4c6, and the feedback on ses_01a1ce2e says again, yet both letters repeat the pattern. The recorded change history that maintains the rules predates these sessions and added no rule for zero values.",
      "traceIds": [
        "ses_01a1c4c6",
        "ses_01a1c542",
        "ses_01a1ce2e"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "hypotheses": [
    {
      "confidence": "high",
      "contradictions": [
        "No feedback states whether the label should stay with a blank value instead of the whole row going, and no letter in the batch shows the alternative, so removal of the whole row is the reading taken from the words leave the line out and drop the line."
      ],
      "statement": "A rule that leaves the sign-on bonus line and the equity line out whenever the approval shows zero for that field would remove the rejected behavior, the same way the existing relocation rule already removes a block that does not apply. All three rejections come from filling a fixed template row with a zero value, and the one letter whose values were non-zero passed with the same filling process unchanged.",
      "traceIds": [
        "ses_01a1c4c6",
        "ses_01a1c542",
        "ses_01a1ce2e",
        "ses_01a1c5f4"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Across four offer letter sessions the agent produced correctly dated, named and formatted letters, and the one session whose approval had real values for every compensation row was accepted. The other three sessions each saved a compensation row filled with a zero the feedback says to leave out: a £0 sign-on bonus line, a 0 shares equity line, and in one session both. The user rejected each of those letters and the wording of the later feedback points back to the earlier ones, so the defect repeated after it was already named. The current rules cover dates, relocation blocks, formatting, file conversion and missing fields, but say nothing about a zero-valued compensation row, so the fixed template rows were treated as mandatory even though the agent's own verification output printed the zeros. In one of the three rejected sessions the agent rebuilt the letter filling from raw document markup instead of the document library the other sessions used, which cost several failed attempts that were all repaired before the reply, and left one extra blank paragraph where the relocation block had been; no feedback mentions that blank space.",
  "unresolved": [
    "Whether the one extra blank paragraph left in the Amara Osei letter after the relocation removal matters to the user: no feedback in the batch mentions blank space, the template itself contributes one blank line in that spot in every letter, and the current rule about removed blocks does not spell out the spacer paragraphs around them.",
    "Whether a zero value in a field other than the sign-on bonus and the equity grant should also leave its line out: only these two compensation rows appear with zeros in the batch, and the feedback names only them."
  ],
  "version": 1
}

artifacts.proposal.json:
{
  "edits": [
    {
      "evidence": [
        "ses_01a1c4c6",
        "ses_01a1c542",
        "ses_01a1ce2e"
      ],
      "newText": "- When a conditional block such as `{{IF_RELOCATION}}…{{END_IF_RELOCATION}}` does not apply, delete the whole paragraph and the heading above it. Never leave an empty paragraph or a heading with nothing under it.\n- When the hiring approval shows no sign-on bonus or no equity grant, for example `£0` or `0 shares`, leave that line out of the letter entirely, the same way a conditional block that does not apply is removed. Never write `£0` for the sign-on bonus or `0 shares` for the equity grant.",
      "oldText": "- When a conditional block such as `{{IF_RELOCATION}}…{{END_IF_RELOCATION}}` does not apply, delete the whole paragraph and the heading above it. Never leave an empty paragraph or a heading with nothing under it.",
      "rationale": "The rules cover non-applicable conditional blocks but made the fixed sign-on bonus and equity rows look mandatory, so three letters kept a zero-valued row and each was rejected for it. This removes those two lines whenever the approval shows no value for them, in the same terms as the existing conditional-block rule."
    }
  ],
  "note": "One rule was added because three letters kept a bonus or equity line the approval valued at zero and each was rejected for it. A sentence about checking the saved letter before replying and a ban reaching every compensation row were left out because no feedback or session shows a missing check or a zero in any other row.",
  "commit_title": "Offer letters: leave out zero sign-on bonus and equity lines",
  "commit_body": "Added an Offer letters rule that leaves the sign-on bonus or equity line out when the hiring approval shows no value for it, such as £0 or 0 shares, removing that line the same way as a conditional block that does not apply, and never writing £0 for the sign-on bonus or 0 shares for the equity grant. In ses_01a1c4c6 the letter kept a £0 sign-on bonus line and the feedback asked to leave the line out, in ses_01a1c542 the letter kept a 0 shares equity line and the feedback asked to drop the line, and in ses_01a1ce2e the letter kept both zero rows and the feedback rejected it again."
}
